WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.530
PAUL JAY: Welcome back to Reality Asserts
Itself. I'm Paul Jay, and this is The Real

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:11.990
News Network.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:16.939
We're continuing our series of interviews
with John Kiriakou. We're at his house, as

00:00:16.939 --> 00:00:22.009
I've said before. He's--continues to be under
house arrest as we do these interviews.

00:00:22.009 --> 00:00:27.660
John was the first CIA officer to officially
confirm that there was in fact a waterboarding

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:32.210
torture program. He's also the only person
that went to jail for anything to do with

00:00:32.210 --> 00:00:37.220
the waterboarding torture program, and that
was because he was the first person to officially

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:41.879
confirm there was a waterboarding torture
program. Unfortunately, the people who were

00:00:41.879 --> 00:00:48.720
actually doing the waterboarding and ordering
the waterboarding didn't go to jail and never

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:50.229
kept him any company in jail.

00:00:50.229 --> 00:00:50.970
Thanks for joining us.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:52.269
JOHN C. KIRIAKOU: Thanks for having me.

00:00:52.269 --> 00:00:57.030
JAY: So let's pick up our story from that.
Why did the CIA target you?

00:00:57.030 --> 00:01:03.900
KIRIAKOU: The CIA never forgive me for confirming
that it was waterboarding and otherwise torturing

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:08.799
prisoners, that that torture was official
U.S. government policy, and that the policy

00:01:08.799 --> 00:01:11.030
was approved by the president. Never forgive
me.

00:01:11.030 --> 00:01:13.170
JAY: So where did you say this, and how did
you get [incompr.]

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:20.170
KIRIAKOU: I said this in December 2007 on
ABC News. And it started oddly for me. I got

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:24.680
a call from Brian Ross.

00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.640
JAY: Let me just back up one sec, 'cause we've
been kind of doing your story chronologically;

00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:29.860
now we're kind of jumping.

00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:30.340
KIRIAKOU: Okay.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.390
JAY: So if I understand correctly, you left
the CIA in what year?

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:34.170
KIRIAKOU: In 2004.

00:01:34.170 --> 00:01:35.550
JAY: Two thousand and four.

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:35.870
KIRIAKOU: Mhm.

00:01:35.870 --> 00:01:38.880
JAY: And you did some consulting. You did
consulting for ABC.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:44.010
KIRIAKOU: Not yet. I left the CIA in 2004
and I joined Deloitte &amp; Touche, which is one

00:01:44.010 --> 00:01:47.070
of the big four public accounting firms.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.530
JAY: Okay. So, first of all, why did you leave
the CIA?

00:01:49.530 --> 00:01:53.970
KIRIAKOU: I left the CIA, as funny as it might
sound, to spend more time with my children.

00:01:53.970 --> 00:02:00.970
I had gone through a very acrimonious divorce.
My ex-wife had moved to Northeastern Ohio

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:05.920
with my two sons so that she could be closer
to her parents. And I knew that my career

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:11.110
was going to take me back overseas again in
short order, and to someplace not family-friendly--Iraq,

00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:18.099
Afghanistan, Pakistan, Yemen, something like
that. And so I decided I had had enough.

00:02:18.099 --> 00:02:25.099
And I had a friend who had a friend at Deloitte
&amp; Touche, one of the big four accounting firms.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:32.420
They were looking for someone to head counter--not
counterintelligence, but competitive intelligence

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:39.420
in their Washington, D.C., office. And I applied
for the job, and a couple of weeks later was

00:02:39.999 --> 00:02:44.790
made an offer. I resigned from the CIA, and
the very next day went into Deloitte &amp; Touche.

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:47.730
JAY: Why does Deloitte Touche want a CIA guy?

00:02:47.730 --> 00:02:52.959
KIRIAKOU: Well, I was actually the fourth
CIA guy that Deloitte &amp; Touche had hired.

00:02:52.959 --> 00:02:59.480
They had created this group called the Competitive
Intelligence Group, with the idea being that

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:06.480
they would hire former CIA officers or other
people with experience in intelligence collection,

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:12.870
and they would pair those people with the
analysts, the corporate research analysts,

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:17.409
to collect and analyze intelligence on their
competitors.

00:03:17.409 --> 00:03:24.409
So my job was to collect first-person human-source
intelligence on Ernst &amp; Young, KPMG, PricewaterhouseCoopers,

00:03:27.409 --> 00:03:34.409
and a myriad of consulting firms, like IBM
and Accenture and others. And we would collect

00:03:36.859 --> 00:03:39.389
this information. Sometimes they were tips.

00:03:39.389 --> 00:03:40.419
JAY: For example?

00:03:40.419 --> 00:03:45.840
KIRIAKOU: Well, for example, we tried to steal
their pricing models or how they determined

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:51.739
discounts. Or, interestingly, at least to
me, we tried to figure out which of their

00:03:51.739 --> 00:03:56.329
partners were disgruntled so we could steal
them, provided that they were top producers.

00:03:56.329 --> 00:03:59.919
JAY: And I'm assuming they're all doing it
back to Deloitte Touche, everyone's doing

00:03:59.919 --> 00:04:00.409
this to each other.

00:04:00.409 --> 00:04:03.699
KIRIAKOU: Absolutely. They all have the same
groups. Now, I will say that Deloitte's no

00:04:03.699 --> 00:04:09.769
longer exists. It went away during the recession.
But the other consulting firms, yes, they

00:04:09.769 --> 00:04:11.180
do have similar groups.

00:04:11.180 --> 00:04:14.029
JAY: And are they looking for dirt on individuals?

00:04:14.029 --> 00:04:14.569
KIRIAKOU: No. No.

00:04:14.569 --> 00:04:16.290
JAY: Or this is more about recruitment?

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:21.690
KIRIAKOU: It's much more about recruitment.
And it's also about undercutting pricing.

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:28.690
If you're going to go into a bid with a price
of $24 million, let's say, we want to know

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:35.870
that so we can go in with a bid of $20 million
and win the job, because a $20 million contract

00:04:37.150 --> 00:04:40.159
of course is better than a zero dollar contract.

00:04:40.159 --> 00:04:45.610
JAY: And so they're all doing it to each other.
So this is a lot about the bidding process,

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:48.830
then, trying to get someone to spill the beans
on a bid.

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:50.229
KIRIAKOU: Yes. The bidding process--.

00:04:50.229 --> 00:04:51.409
JAY: It's like recruiting a double agent.

00:04:51.409 --> 00:04:56.539
KIRIAKOU: Yeah. It's the bidding process.
It's discounts. It's disgruntled partners,

00:04:56.539 --> 00:05:03.539
like I said. It's plans to expand service
lines or offerings. Anything that might give

00:05:04.349 --> 00:05:05.819
you a corporate leg up.

00:05:05.819 --> 00:05:07.960
JAY: And were you successful?

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:10.159
KIRIAKOU: Wildly, in some cases.

00:05:10.159 --> 00:05:13.419
JAY: Really? Alright. That's--we'll do that
another day.

00:05:13.419 --> 00:05:14.560
KIRIAKOU: Okay.

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:20.620
JAY: Okay. So then what? Deloitte Touche.
At what point do you start consulting for

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:21.080
ABC?

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:27.020
KIRIAKOU: Not until after I went on TV and
talked about torture. I was at my desk at

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:32.289
Deloitte one day and I got a call from Brian
Ross at ABC News. And Brian Ross told me that

00:05:32.289 --> 00:05:38.970
he had a source who said that I had tortured
Abu Zubaydah. I said that was absolutely untrue,

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:43.860
that I was the only person who was ever kind
to Abu Zubaydah, and I said I never have laid

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:49.159
a hand on anybody, least of all Abu Zubaydah.
And he said--and I learned later that this

00:05:49.159 --> 00:05:53.349
was an old reporter's trick--he said, well,
you can come on the show and defend yourself.

00:05:53.349 --> 00:06:00.280
In the meantime, that same day, the day before,
the same week, whatever it was, President

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:04.340
Bush was telling the press that we do not
torture, we do not torture, we do not torture.

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:11.340
GEORGE W. BUSH: This government does not torture
people. You know, we stick to U.S. law and

00:06:12.219 --> 00:06:15.539
our international obligations.

00:06:15.539 --> 00:06:22.539
Thirdly, there are highly trained professionals
questioning these extremists and terrorists.

00:06:25.110 --> 00:06:30.840
We've got professionals who are trained in
this kind of work to get information that'll

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.189
protect the American people.

00:06:32.189 --> 00:06:37.629
And by the way, we have gotten information
from these high-value detainees that have

00:06:37.629 --> 00:06:39.039
helped protect you.

00:06:39.039 --> 00:06:42.240
KIRIAKOU: I knew that was a lie.

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.919
And then, later, he said that we don't torture,
but if there is torture, it's the result of

00:06:47.919 --> 00:06:53.250
a rogue CIA officer or a rogue operation.
I knew that was a lie.

00:06:53.250 --> 00:07:00.250
So I went on ABC news. And he never even asked
me on camera if I had tortured Abu Zubaydah.

00:07:01.139 --> 00:07:03.030
He just went straight into the torture.

00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:08.159
KIRIAKOU: And at the time, I felt that waterboarding
was something we needed to do. And as time

00:07:08.159 --> 00:07:15.159
has passed and as September 11 has moved farther
and farther back into history, I think I've

00:07:16.310 --> 00:07:20.360
changed my mind and I think that waterboarding's
probably something that we shouldn't be in

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:20.780
the business of doing.

00:07:20.780 --> 00:07:22.729
BRIAN ROSS, JOURNALIST, ABC NEWS: Why do you
say that now?

00:07:22.729 --> 00:07:25.900
KIRIAKOU: Because we're Americans and we're
better than that.

00:07:25.900 --> 00:07:28.360
ROSS: But at the time, you didn't feel that
way.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:34.749
KIRIAKOU: At the time, I was so angry and
I wanted so much to help disrupt future attacks

00:07:34.749 --> 00:07:38.009
on the United States that I felt it was the
only thing we could do.

00:07:38.009 --> 00:07:39.930
ROSS: And with Zubaydah you think that was
successful.

00:07:39.930 --> 00:07:40.849
KIRIAKOU: It was.

00:07:40.849 --> 00:07:44.379
ROSS: And we have reported that Khalid Sheikh
Mohammed was also waterboarded.

00:07:44.379 --> 00:07:48.749
KIRIAKOU: I was out of it by then, but it's
my understanding that he was also waterboarded.

00:07:48.749 --> 00:07:50.080
ROSS: But those were really the only two.

00:07:50.080 --> 00:07:52.030
KIRIAKOU: To the best of my knowledge, yes.

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:54.939
ROSS: And bottom line, as you sit here now,
do you think that was worth it?

00:07:54.939 --> 00:07:55.349
KIRIAKOU: Yes.

00:07:55.349 --> 00:07:59.110
ROSS: Did it compromise American principles,
or did it save American lives, or both?

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:03.419
KIRIAKOU: It--I think both. It may have compromised
our principles, at least in the short term.

00:08:03.419 --> 00:08:06.810
And I think it's good that we're having a
national debate about this. We should be debating

00:08:06.810 --> 00:08:10.639
this and Congress should be talking about
it, because I think as a country we have to

00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:14.520
decide if this is something that we want to
do as a matter of policy. I'm not saying now

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:19.430
that we should, but at the very least we should
be talking about it. It shouldn't be secret.

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:21.900
It should be out there as part of the national
debate.

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:25.650
KIRIAKOU: In that interview, I essentially
called the president of the United States

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:28.969
a liar. In fact, the next day, at a press
conference--.

00:08:28.969 --> 00:08:33.260
DANA PERINO: I am saying that the United States
does not torture. The president has been--.

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:36.979
JOURNALIST: I'm asking you if what was done
in this case was not torture, in your opinion.

00:08:36.979 --> 00:08:39.340
PERINO: I'm saying the United States does
not torture.

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:44.280
JOURNALIST: But we have a former CIA officer,
John Kiriakou, now saying that waterboarding

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:48.780
was used. And you're saying the interrogations
were legal. He's saying on the record now

00:08:48.780 --> 00:08:53.530
waterboarding was used in at least one case.
You're saying waterboarding is legal?

00:08:53.530 --> 00:08:57.740
PERINO: Ed, I'm saying I'm not commenting
on any specific technique. I'm not commenting

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:02.160
on that gentleman's characteristics of any
possible technique. I have given you a very

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:06.570
general statement about interrogations being
legal, limited [crosstalk]

00:09:06.570 --> 00:09:06.650
JOURNALIST: [crosstalk] was legal.

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:06.710
PERINO: I'm sorry?

00:09:06.710 --> 00:09:08.310
JOURNALIST: You said--was it in the legal
framework?

00:09:08.310 --> 00:09:09.180
PERINO: Yes.

00:09:09.180 --> 00:09:11.360
JOURNALIST: Everything that was done?

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:12.230
PERINO: Yes.

00:09:12.230 --> 00:09:13.750
JOURNALIST: So waterboarding is legal.

00:09:13.750 --> 00:09:17.190
PERINO: I'm not commenting on any specific
techniques. And you can ask me all sorts of

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:20.280
different ways and we can go back and forth,
but I'm not going to do it, Ed.

00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:25.560
KIRIAKOU: Well, immediately the CIA filed
what's called a crimes report with the Justice

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:32.130
Department, alleging that I had violated my
secrecy agreement by leaking classified information.

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:38.810
The FBI investigated, they watched the tape,
and they concluded that it was already common

00:09:38.810 --> 00:09:43.350
knowledge that we were torturing prisoners.
Human Rights Watch had come out with a report.

00:09:43.350 --> 00:09:48.820
Amnesty International had come out with a
report. So I had added nothing, technically,

00:09:48.820 --> 00:09:50.460
to the debate.

00:09:50.460 --> 00:09:57.460
But what I had done was I had become the first
CIA officer, current or former, to actually

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:02.170
confirm that we were torturing prisoners and
that torture was our policy.

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:06.220
JAY: Now, when you first talked about it,
you defend torture, do you not? There's a

00:10:06.220 --> 00:10:09.460
point where you say he's been tortured, waterboarded
once.

00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:14.630
KIRIAKOU: A lot of people think that I was
defending it. In fact, I was trying to be

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:20.820
nuanced, and I failed at being nuanced. I
was trying to present it as two questions,

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:27.230
two separate issues. One, did it work? And
two, was it right? And I said it wasn't right,

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:31.200
it was wrong morally and ethically, that it
was torture and that I was opposed to it.

00:10:31.200 --> 00:10:35.100
In fact, I had been asked by a senior officer
in the Counterterrorism Center if I wanted

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:39.620
to be certified in the use of these so-called
enhanced interrogation techniques, and I declined.

00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:42.580
I said I had a moral problem with it.

00:10:42.580 --> 00:10:48.380
On that first question, did it work, the CIA
officers in the field who were torturing prisoners

00:10:48.380 --> 00:10:54.330
were reporting back that it was working and
that Abu Zubaydah, for example, had cracked

00:10:54.330 --> 00:11:00.130
after one session, one waterboarding session,
and that he had given up actionable information

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:01.240
that saved American lives.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:04.790
JAY: And that's what--when you're on television,
that's what you knew at that time.

00:11:04.790 --> 00:11:05.870
KIRIAKOU: That's what I knew at that time.

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:08.710
JAY: So you conclude that it was done once
and it worked.

00:11:08.710 --> 00:11:14.690
KIRIAKOU: Correct; although it was wrong,
it had worked. We didn't know, nobody knew,

00:11:14.690 --> 00:11:21.000
until the CIA inspector general report was
released in April 2009 that said that, no,

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:27.640
he was waterboarded 83 times and Khalid Sheikh
Mohammed was waterboarded 147 times. So they

00:11:27.640 --> 00:11:33.170
were even lying to each other internally to
try to justify this thing.

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:39.390
So I told Brian Ross what I thought I knew
as facts, but I was wrong.

00:11:39.390 --> 00:11:45.320
JAY: So what's the next big beat for you?
Is it the inspector general's report?

00:11:45.320 --> 00:11:52.320
KIRIAKOU: Well, yeah. In that year, 2008 to
early 2009, I ended up consulting for ABC

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:56.560
News. And, frankly, what I had done is I called
Brian Ross and I said, you ruined my life;

00:11:56.560 --> 00:12:00.500
the least you could do is throw me a little
bit of work. And so ABC brought me on as a

00:12:00.500 --> 00:12:02.690
consultant.

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:08.220
In early 2009, John Kerry offered me a job
as the senior investigator on the Senate Foreign

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:14.500
Relations Committee. So I was under the impression
that he actually wanted me to investigate

00:12:14.500 --> 00:12:19.430
things. And I ran into even more trouble with
the CIA in that job.

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:26.430
JAY: Now, between going on TV and acknowledging
that there is torture, they open a file on

00:12:26.870 --> 00:12:33.640
you, and the inspector general's report, and
Kerry's job, how actively are they pursuing

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:35.290
you and how aware are you of it?

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:42.290
KIRIAKOU: I was unaware of any investigation
aimed at me, targeting me. It turned out that

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:49.140
they never stopped investigating me from December
2007 until my arrest in 2012.

00:12:49.140 --> 00:12:56.140
JAY: Prior to you going on television, was
there any sense that the CIA wasn't happy

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:57.120
with you?

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:02.500
KIRIAKOU: Oh, no, none. On the contrary, when
I was at Deloitte &amp; Touche, they were preparing

00:13:02.500 --> 00:13:08.200
to bid for a job in the CIA's Counterterrorism
Center. I still had an active security clearance

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:13.200
at the time, and so they asked if they could
add me to the list of cleared individuals

00:13:13.200 --> 00:13:20.200
that might participate in this contract. I
said yes, and I called the security officer

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:24.920
at the Counterterrorism Center, and she said,
yeah, that's great with us. And I ended up

00:13:24.920 --> 00:13:28.930
not doing it, because my boss was willing
to give me up for six weeks, but they wanted

00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:33.390
me for six months until the contract really
got going. But I had no problems whatsoever

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:34.710
with the CIA until I went on ABC.

00:13:34.710 --> 00:13:41.480
JAY: 'Cause you'd think if they had no issues
with you and you say this one thing on TV,

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:45.560
they would say, oh, look, this guy's been
a stellar agent all this time and never--and

00:13:45.560 --> 00:13:50.510
so on, and everyone already knows it's happening;
we could just, like, ignore it.

00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:50.870
KIRIAKOU: Right.

00:13:50.870 --> 00:13:55.840
JAY: But by targeting you, they're making
it 100 times more an issue, even in the public

00:13:55.840 --> 00:13:59.720
eye. And eventually it's going to go public.
I mean, look where it's led to, all these

00:13:59.720 --> 00:14:00.290
interviews [crosstalk]

00:14:00.290 --> 00:14:04.680
KIRIAKOU: Well, that's why I maintain the
CIA is the organization that made me a human

00:14:04.680 --> 00:14:11.680
rights activist. It's the CIA that's made
me want to take to the streets to defend our

00:14:11.970 --> 00:14:16.550
civil liberties. Otherwise, I wouldn't have
given it two minutes of thought. But you're

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:17.520
exactly right.

00:14:17.520 --> 00:14:24.520
And even if they were angry, there are other
cases of CIA officers, current CIA officers,

00:14:24.620 --> 00:14:28.890
exposing classified information. And what
happens to them is they get a letter in their

00:14:28.890 --> 00:14:34.250
file and they don't get promoted for a year.
They don't get seven felony charges--or five

00:14:34.250 --> 00:14:34.720
felony charges.

00:14:34.720 --> 00:14:38.550
JAY: So I go back to my first question: why
target you in this way?

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:44.700
KIRIAKOU: I think that the targeting came
from inside the Counterterrorism Center. It

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:51.700
wasn't necessarily the CIA as an organization,
as a whole; it was CTC. CTC was run by cowboys

00:14:53.730 --> 00:15:00.730
and hardasses. And I think that they were
so furious, that what I had done was unforgivable,

00:15:02.890 --> 00:15:06.460
that they are the ones who asked the Justice
Department to go after me.

00:15:06.460 --> 00:15:09.440
JAY: CTC is this thing we see on this TV show
24, right?

00:15:09.440 --> 00:15:09.960
KIRIAKOU: Yes.

00:15:09.960 --> 00:15:13.930
JAY: That's--I mean, does it bear any resemblance
to reality, the 24 room?

00:15:13.930 --> 00:15:19.000
KIRIAKOU: No. The room? No. No, that's all
Hollywood.

00:15:19.000 --> 00:15:24.560
JAY: Okay. So let's jump ahead. The inspector
general's report comes out. You find out that

00:15:24.560 --> 00:15:26.000
it wasn't once; it was 83 times.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:26.350
KIRIAKOU: Right.

00:15:26.350 --> 00:15:32.230
JAY: And in fact the intelligence wasn't actionable.
So what does that do to you? And what are

00:15:32.230 --> 00:15:32.890
you doing at the time?

00:15:32.890 --> 00:15:35.760
KIRIAKOU: Well, frankly, it makes me look
like a fool. At the time, I was working for

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:40.110
John Kerry. I had only been there about a
month, maybe six weeks. But it made me look

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:43.070
like a fool.

00:15:43.070 --> 00:15:50.070
But I issued a statement saying I gave the
facts as I knew them. I think that the inspector

00:15:50.130 --> 00:15:57.130
general's report shows that the CIA will lie
to its own staff officers. I had no regrets.

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:05.850
JAY: Two years had gone by. So why does this
eventually lead to a charge? I don't get--I

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:06.850
mean, how--.

00:16:06.850 --> 00:16:11.340
KIRIAKOU: While I was at the Senate Foreign
Relations Committee, I decided to do some

00:16:11.340 --> 00:16:18.340
real investigations. So at first I wanted
to investigate what was called the Dasht-i-Leili

00:16:19.870 --> 00:16:26.870
massacre. This was an incident in northern
Afghanistan on November 30 and December 1,

00:16:28.610 --> 00:16:35.610
2001, where the Northern Alliance had captured
as many as 2,000 Taliban soldiers and wanted

00:16:36.690 --> 00:16:42.800
to incarcerate them. Well, there were no jails
in Afghanistan big enough to hold 2,000 prisoners

00:16:42.800 --> 00:16:49.280
all at once. So what the Northern Alliance
decided to do was to load these men onto container

00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:54.940
trucks and take them out into the desert,
deep into the desert, and just hold them there

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:57.800
until they could figure out how to get them
to different jails around the country.

00:16:57.800 --> 00:16:58.730
JAY: This is General Dostum.

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:00.530
KIRIAKOU: This is General Dostum and his men.

00:17:00.530 --> 00:17:02.000
JAY: Who's now a vice president?

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:09.000
KIRIAKOU: Yeah, vice president or deputy prime
minister, or one or the other. So nobody had

00:17:09.750 --> 00:17:14.470
put any air holes in these containers, and
there was no food and there was no water.

00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:17.550
So after an eight-hour drive into the desert--.

00:17:17.550 --> 00:17:19.589
JAY: How many people were packed in a container?

00:17:19.589 --> 00:17:23.920
KIRIAKOU: Two thousand people in a series
of containers. So, after eight hours' drive

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:28.220
into the desert, one of the witnesses who
survived told us that when they opened the

00:17:28.220 --> 00:17:35.220
back of the tracks, that bodies fell out like
sardines out of a can. So almost everybody

00:17:35.360 --> 00:17:40.550
died. By our count, there were 18 survivors
out of 2,000.

00:17:40.550 --> 00:17:45.430
The reason I was interested in this is because
I had taken a call from a human rights activist

00:17:45.430 --> 00:17:52.430
who told us that he had a witness, who was
a boy at the time and didn't go onto the truck,

00:17:52.480 --> 00:17:58.680
and he said that the boy saw two white men
wearing jeans and black T-shirts and speaking

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:05.490
English at the box-up. Well, who's going to
be in Mazari Sharif, Afghanistan, speaking

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:10.670
English, wearing bluejeans, on November 30,
2001?

00:18:10.670 --> 00:18:17.670
So I interviewed everybody at the State Department
at the time who mattered from 2001. I interviewed

00:18:19.120 --> 00:18:26.120
several human rights activists. And I sent
a letter to the agency asking for some clarification.

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:34.020
Within a couple of days, the deputy office
director--or staff director is what he's called--at

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:38.760
the Senate Foreign Relations Committee told
me that the investigation was dead and to

00:18:38.760 --> 00:18:39.820
drop it.

00:18:39.820 --> 00:18:45.160
JAY: Just quickly, just coincidentally, I
happened to have made a documentary called

00:18:45.160 --> 00:18:50.780
Return to Kandahar in Afghanistan in the spring
of 2002, and we interviewed General Dostum.

00:18:50.780 --> 00:18:55.520
So here's a few seconds of that, just to give
you a sense of who we're talking about here.

00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:52.500
NELOFER PAZIRA: In the '90s, when these guys fought each other,

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:59.800
over 1 million people died and half of the city of Kabul was destroyed. That's not a football game.

00:20:00.600 --> 00:20:06.500
JAY: So that's General Dostum. Now, General Dostum clearly is--was, is a war criminal.

00:20:06.500 --> 00:20:12.240
You had uncovered evidence that proved it.
And it gets shut down. Why?

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:18.990
KIRIAKOU: I think that I was poking the hornets
nest at the time. And I'm not sure if I was

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:24.460
doing it subconsciously or if it just worked
out that way. But I wanted to know if CIA

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:29.280
officers were present at the box-up. Now,
I know CIA officers were present 20 miles

00:20:29.280 --> 00:20:34.309
away, because the box-up was the same day
as the uprising at Qala-i-Jangi, where--

00:20:34.309 --> 00:20:35.590
JAY: This is the prison.

00:20:35.590 --> 00:20:41.400
KIRIAKOU: --Mike Spann was killed, in the
Taliban - al-Qaeda uprising that took place

00:20:41.400 --> 00:20:44.890
in that prison. So I know they were in the
area. I wanted to know if they were at the

00:20:44.890 --> 00:20:45.020
box-up.

00:20:45.020 --> 00:20:46.040
JAY: Which was another slaughter.

00:20:46.040 --> 00:20:48.870
KIRIAKOU: It was another slaughter, yes.

00:20:48.870 --> 00:20:50.809
So I dropped that investigation.

00:20:50.809 --> 00:20:55.070
And then I got a call from a reporter who
told me that he had some information that

00:20:55.070 --> 00:21:00.350
seemed to indicate that the CIA was taking
its torturers and putting them under State

00:21:00.350 --> 00:21:01.270
Department cover.

00:21:01.270 --> 00:21:05.430
JAY: Let's back up one sec just to close the
loop on the previous story. If you had in

00:21:05.430 --> 00:21:10.800
fact found that there were CIA at the box-up,
they would have been implicated in a war crime.

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:15.490
KIRIAKOU: Correct. Correct. And that war crime
would have been detailed in an official report

00:21:15.490 --> 00:21:17.460
from the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.

00:21:17.460 --> 00:21:19.050
JAY: Okay.

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:24.050
KIRIAKOU: So I moved on to another investigation.
A reporter called me and said that he had

00:21:24.050 --> 00:21:29.059
information that the CIA was taking some of
its torturers and placing them under State

00:21:29.059 --> 00:21:34.110
Department cover, which was a violation of
the cover agreement between the CIA and the

00:21:34.110 --> 00:21:40.510
State Department. So I sent the State Department
a letter asking for clarification.

00:21:40.510 --> 00:21:45.380
Weeks passed. Something like five, six weeks
passed. And finally a colleague of mine comes

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:50.970
into my office and says, oh, you got a response
to your letter from the agency. I said, I

00:21:50.970 --> 00:21:55.670
didn't get any response. He said, well, they
classified it top secret, and you're not cleared

00:21:55.670 --> 00:22:02.100
for it. I said, well, what'd they say? And
he smiles and he says, they said go fuck yourself.

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:04.200
So that was the end of that investigation.

00:22:04.200 --> 00:22:06.870
JAY: So how long after all this do you get
charged?

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:13.870
KIRIAKOU: Well, it was another three years.
There were other investigations, too, at which

00:22:13.880 --> 00:22:19.970
I was poking them. They even called my wife
in to the CIA security office and threatened

00:22:19.970 --> 00:22:20.120
her.

00:22:20.120 --> 00:22:20.980
JAY: Now, your wife--.

00:22:20.980 --> 00:22:22.650
KIRIAKOU: My wife was a CIA officer at the
time.

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:23.260
JAY: Is or was?

00:22:23.260 --> 00:22:23.700
KIRIAKOU: Was.

00:22:23.700 --> 00:22:25.320
JAY: Was CIA. You met at the CIA.

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:26.309
KIRIAKOU: We met at the CIA.

00:22:26.309 --> 00:22:27.680
JAY: So they call her in and what?

00:22:27.680 --> 00:22:32.330
KIRIAKOU: Well, I had written an op-ed in
the Los Angeles Times in which I said that

00:22:32.330 --> 00:22:38.430
we need to be very cautious about Iran's inroads
into Latin America; we need to improve our

00:22:38.430 --> 00:22:45.430
relations with Latin American countries because
the Iranians are improving theirs. And I wrote

00:22:45.980 --> 00:22:52.960
one sentence in that op-ed dealing with Iranian
travel to Latin America.

00:22:52.960 --> 00:22:56.380
Now, I got that op-ed cleared by the CIA.

00:22:56.380 --> 00:23:03.380
So, finally, they call my wife in to security.
And they have a camera on. And the security

00:23:05.010 --> 00:23:10.450
guy says, this is not an investigation, it's
an inquiry, but we're going to tape it. What

00:23:10.450 --> 00:23:15.590
do you think should be done with people who
leak classified information? (Totally loaded

00:23:15.590 --> 00:23:19.750
question.) She said, well, what are you talking
about? Well, your husband wrote an op-ed in

00:23:19.750 --> 00:23:23.210
the Los Angeles Times. And she said, he got
that op-ed cleared.

00:23:23.210 --> 00:23:26.640
JAY: Let me ask, why would you have to get
it cleared? You're no longer working for the

00:23:26.640 --> 00:23:32.559
KIRIAKOU: I have to get anything I write dealing
with intelligence, CIA, foreign affairs, government,

00:23:32.559 --> 00:23:36.920
anything, for the rest of my life, cleared
by the CIA's Publications Review Board.

00:23:36.920 --> 00:23:39.309
JAY: What about this interview or the other
interviews [crosstalk]

00:23:39.309 --> 00:23:46.309
KIRIAKOU: Well, these are written products--articles,
books, magazine articles, you know, op-eds.

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:48.309
JAY: Interviews are okay.

00:23:48.309 --> 00:23:49.270
KIRIAKOU: Interviews are okay.

00:23:49.270 --> 00:23:50.740
JAY: Alright. Go on.

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:57.710
KIRIAKOU: So she said, well, he got that cleared
by the Publications Review Board. And the

00:23:57.710 --> 00:24:04.710
guy tells her, and we really appreciate that,
but there is a line in there that is top-secret.

00:24:07.290 --> 00:24:10.970
And she said, well, that's impossible; my
husband doesn't have access to top-secret

00:24:10.970 --> 00:24:16.910
information. And he says, but you do. So she
said, I think I've said enough. And she got

00:24:16.910 --> 00:24:17.920
up and walked away.

00:24:17.920 --> 00:24:19.200
JAY: What was the line?

00:24:19.200 --> 00:24:23.690
KIRIAKOU: Well, it was that Iranians no longer
need visas to travel to Bolivia.

00:24:23.690 --> 00:24:26.610
JAY: What?! This was top-secret?

00:24:26.610 --> 00:24:31.260
KIRIAKOU: She was very upset. And she called
me. And I told her, take it easy. I said,

00:24:31.260 --> 00:24:35.350
do you know where I got that information?
And she said, no. I said, I got it from the

00:24:35.350 --> 00:24:38.640
Bolivian Foreign Ministry's website. It's
on the website.

00:24:38.640 --> 00:24:41.280
JAY: And why on earth would it be secret anyway?

00:24:41.280 --> 00:24:46.980
KIRIAKOU: I told her, one thing that you may
want to tell them is, number one, apparently

00:24:46.980 --> 00:24:52.770
nobody in the CIA speaks Spanish, first of
all. Secondly, they apparently have a source

00:24:52.770 --> 00:24:59.090
who's selling them press and calling it intelligence.
So they were upset about that.

00:24:59.090 --> 00:25:00.730
JAY: Well, they're out to get you at this
point.

00:25:00.730 --> 00:25:06.370
KIRIAKOU: At that point they were out to get
me. But I didn't think that this would result

00:25:06.370 --> 00:25:08.620
in any kind of criminal charges.

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:15.370
JAY: It's almost sounding like when you go
public on the torture issue, they open a file.

00:25:15.370 --> 00:25:16.030
KIRIAKOU: Yeah.

00:25:16.030 --> 00:25:19.679
JAY: It's this stuff where they really decide,
we have to shut this guy up.

00:25:19.679 --> 00:25:25.760
KIRIAKOU: Well, it got much, much worse. Toward
the end of my stay at the Senate Foreign Relations

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:28.540
Committee, there was an incident that I'll
tell you about.

00:25:28.540 --> 00:25:29.809
Part of my job was to have--.

00:25:29.809 --> 00:25:33.260
JAY: Okay. Hang on. This is going to be a
cliffhanger. You've got to join us for the

00:25:33.260 --> 00:25:38.610
next part to find out what happened next on
Reality Asserts Itself on The Real News Network.

00:25:38.610 --> 00:25:45.610
Thanks for joining us.

